rom ep-exdos13.rom page 0 cpu z80
default observed

# Range 6: cluster-chain allocation and freeing, EXDOS functions 8-11 (table $0550), and the
# floppy controller driver DISK_IO (also reached from the header's jump at $000D).

@19DD CHAIN_ADD_LOOP
  Allocation loop: keeps the clusters still to allocate and the last cluster of the chain on the
  stack, then searches for the next free cluster (CHAIN_FIND_FREE).
  .19DD Clusters still to allocate
  .19DE Last cluster of the chain (1 at the start)

@19DF CHAIN_FIND_FREE
  Scans the FAT upwards from cluster DE + 1 for a free entry (0), links it to the last cluster of
  the chain, clears it when bit 1 of IY+$48 is set, and goes on until the count on the stack is
  used up; then ends the chain with $FFF. A run that reaches the disk's last cluster goes to
  CHAIN_DISK_FULL.
  .19DF Next cluster to try
  .19E0 HL = the drive's parameter block
  .19E1 HL+$16 = the cluster count + 2 (one past the last)
  .19E9 Past the last cluster?
  .19EC Yes: the disk is full
  .19EF DE = the cluster's FAT entry
  .19F2 Entry 0 (free)?
  .19F5 No: try the next cluster
  .19F7 BC = the free cluster
  .19F9 DE = the last cluster of the chain
  .19FB Link it to the free cluster
  .19FE DE = the new cluster, now the chain's last
  .1A02 Clear new clusters?
  .1A06 Yes: zero its sectors
  .1A0A BC = clusters still to allocate
  .1A0B One less
  .1A0E More to allocate: search on from this cluster
  .1A10 End of chain mark
  .1A13 Ends the chain at the last new cluster
  .1A16 FAT entry 1 holds the chain's first cluster
  .1A19 DE = it
  .1A1C A = 0: done

@1A1F CHAIN_DISK_FULL
  The disk ran out of free clusters: frees every cluster this call allocated (the chain hung on FAT
  entry 1) and leaves with error $AC (Disk full) through CHAIN_RESTORE_1.
  .1A1F Drops the last cluster of the chain
  .1A20 Drops the count still to allocate
  .1A21 The chain starts at FAT entry 1
  .1A24 Frees entry 1 and the clusters linked from it
  .1A27 Error code $AC (Disk full)

@1A29 CHAIN_RESTORE_1
  The end of the allocation that starts at $19CB: puts back FAT entry 1, which the allocation used
  as the head of the new chain, and returns. BC = the value entry 1 held (the first new cluster on
  success), A = 0 with Z on success or the error code with NZ; DE = the caller's DE.
  .1A29 BC = entry 1's value before the allocation
  .1A2A Keeps the result code
  .1A2B DE = entry 1's value now (the chain's head)
  .1A2C FAT entry 1
  .1A2F Puts its old value back
  .1A32 BC = the chain's first cluster
  .1A34 Z when A = 0 (done), NZ for an error
  .1A35 The caller's DE

@1A37 NEW_CLUSTER_ZERO
  Zeroes every sector of a newly allocated cluster, last sector first, through the sector buffers
  (each one marked changed). When bit 2 of IY+$48 is set (a directory is being made) it then writes
  the "." entry (pointing at the cluster itself) and the ".." entry (pointing at the cluster held at
  IY+$30/$31) into the cluster's first sector, both with attributes $12.
  Entry: BC = the cluster; HL = the drive's parameter block (+$0A sectors per cluster); IY = EXDOS's
  variables.
  Exit: the cluster's sectors are zero in the buffers.
  Changes: AF, DE, AF', BC', DE', HL'; the sector buffers.
  Keeps: BC, HL, IX, IY.
  .1A37 Keeps the cluster
  .1A38 DE = the cluster
  .1A3B HL+$0A = sectors per cluster
  .1A3F B = sectors per cluster
  .1A41 A = sector offset: one past the cluster's last
  .1A42 DE = that sector's number
  .1A45 A = sectors per cluster
  .1A46 A = sectors to clear + 1; NZ, so the first pass reads nothing
  .1A47 BC = the cluster
  .1A48 IX = the parameter block, the caller's IX on the stack
  .1A4B Keeps the cluster

@1A4D CLR_SECTOR_LOOP
  Gets a buffer for sector DE (after the first pass, through $09FC) and zeroes its 512 data bytes.
  .1A4D Keeps the count
  .1A4E Not the first pass: calls $09FC first
  .1A51 B = 0
  .1A53 HL = the buffer for sector DE
  .1A56 Keeps the buffer
  .1A57 The data starts after the buffer's 11-byte header
  .1A5B A = 0, B = 0: 256 passes of 2 bytes

@1A5C CLR_FILL
  Zero-fill loop for the buffer's 512 bytes; then moves to the previous sector, and after the last
  writes the "." and ".." entries when a directory is being made.
  .1A5C Zeroes two bytes
  .1A60 512 bytes in all
  .1A62 Marks the buffer changed
  .1A65 HL = the buffer
  .1A66 B = sectors still to clear
  .1A67 The previous sector
  .1A68 Z: later passes call $09FC
  .1A69 Next sector
  .1A6B BC = the cluster
  .1A6C Making a directory?
  .1A70 No: done
  .1A72 DE = the first entry of the first sector
  .1A77 HL = the name "." padded with spaces
  .1A7A Attributes $12
  .1A7C Writes the "." entry, cluster BC
  .1A7F DE = the second entry
  .1A84 HL = the name ".." padded with spaces
  .1A87 BC = the parent directory's cluster
  .1A8D Attributes $12
  .1A8F Writes the ".." entry

@1A92 CLR_EXIT
  Puts back the caller's IX and HL and returns.
  .1A92 IX = the caller's IX
  .1A94 HL = the drive's parameter block

@1AA2 CHAIN_FREE
  Frees a cluster chain: sets the FAT entry of DE and of every cluster linked from it to 0. Stops
  at an entry that was 0 or at the end-of-chain mark ($FFFF from $1148).
  Entry: DE = the first cluster; HL = the drive's parameter block.
  Exit: Z with A = 0 when the last entry was 0; NZ when the chain ended with an end mark.
  Changes: AF, BC, DE, AF', BC', DE', HL'; the FAT.
  Keeps: HL, IX, IY.
  .1AA2 Keeps the cluster
  .1AA3 DE = its FAT entry (the next cluster)
  .1AA6 BC = the cluster
  .1AA7 Keeps the next cluster
  .1AAA Free
  .1AAD Sets the cluster's FAT entry to 0
  .1AB0 DE = the next cluster
  .1AB1 Was the entry 0?
  .1AB4 End mark ($FFFF)?
  .1AB6 No: free the next one

@1AB9 FN8_DELETE
  EXDOS function 8 (table $0550): deletes the file or the empty directory whose descriptor is at DE.
  1. Finds its directory entry ($12F9).
  2. A directory: refuses "." and "..", enters it and checks every entry is ".", ".." or empty,
     then finds the entry again.
  3. Refuses a read-only file, and a file whose first cluster and drive match the ones recorded
     at IY+$60-$62 while bit 1 of IY-$37 is set.
  4. Frees the cluster chain, keeps the name's first character at entry+$0C and marks the entry
     deleted ($E5).
  Entry: DE = the file's descriptor (first byte $FE); HL = passed on to $12F9 and $1268.
  Exit: A = 0 with Z.
  Error: NZ (or C from the "." check) with A = the error code: $9E (Invalid . or .. operations),
  $A5 (Directory not empty), $A7 (Read only file), $95 (File cannot be copied onto itself), or one
  from $12F9.
  Changes: AF, BC, DE, HL, IX, AF', BC', DE', HL'; the directory, the FAT.
  Keeps: IY.
  .1AB9 BC = HL, kept for finding the entry again
  .1ABB DE = the entry, IX = the descriptor, A = attributes
  .1ABE Not found
  .1ABF A directory?
  .1AC1 No: delete the file
  .1AC3 Is it "." or ".."?
  .1AC6 Yes: error $9E
  .1AC7 Enters the directory
  .1ACA Its first entry

@1ACD DEL_DIR_SCAN
  Walks the directory being deleted: any entry other than ".", ".." or an empty one stops with
  error $A5 (Directory not empty).
  .1ACD End of the directory: it is empty
  .1ACF Empty, "." or ".."?
  .1AD2 Error code $A5 (Directory not empty)
  .1AD4 No: the directory has a file
  .1AD5 The next entry
  .1AD8 Not past the end: check it

@1ADA DEL_DIR_EMPTY
  The directory is empty: finds its own entry again from the descriptor and clears the word at
  HL+$1E ($0EC3).
  .1ADA DE = the descriptor
  .1ADD HL = the caller's HL again
  .1ADF Finds the entry again
  .1AE2 Not found
  .1AE3 Clears the word at HL+$1E

@1AE6 DEL_ENTRY
  Deletes the entry: refuses a read-only file, frees the chain and marks the entry deleted.
  .1AE6 Calls $1268 with HL
  .1AF2 Read only?
  .1AF4 Error code $A7 (Read only file)
  .1AF7 Yes: refused
  .1B05 No
  .1B0D No
  .1B15 The same cluster?
  .1B17 Yes: error $95

@1B19 DEL_FREE_MARK
  Frees the file's cluster chain (when it has one) and marks the entry deleted, the old first
  character kept at entry+$0C.
  .1B19 HL = the caller's HL
  .1B1A Any cluster?
  .1B1C Yes: frees the chain
  .1B1F DE = the entry pointer kept at $1AF8
  .1B20 DE = the directory entry
  .1B24 HL = entry+$0C
  .1B28 Keeps the name's first character there
  .1B2A Deleted mark
  .1B2D Marks the buffer changed

@1B33 DEL_IN_USE
  The file matches the one recorded at IY+$60-$62: leaves with error $95 (File cannot be copied
  onto itself), NZ.
  .1B33 Error code $95
  .1B37 NZ: an error

@1B39 IS_DOT_ENTRY
  Tests whether the directory entry at DE is "." or "..", comparing it with the names at $1A97 and
  $1A96 through $1670 (whose matching takes bit 3 of IX+$06 into account).
  Entry: DE = the directory entry; IX = the descriptor.
  Exit: A = $9E (Invalid . or .. operations); C when the entry is "." or "..", else NC. Z when it
  matched or is empty (first byte 0 or $E5), NZ for any other entry.
  Changes: AF.
  Keeps: BC, DE, HL, IX, IY.
  .1B3B HL = "." padded with spaces
  .1B3E Is the entry "."?
  .1B41 HL = ".." padded with spaces
  .1B44 Not ".": is it ".."?
  .1B49 Error code $9E, for a caller that refuses them

@1B4C FN9_RENAME
  EXDOS function 9 (table $0550): renames a file. The new name is taken from the descriptor at BC
  into the file's descriptor (a "?" keeps the old character), checked, and written into the file's
  directory entry.
  1. Finds the entry ($12F9), clears the word at HL+$1E for a directory ($0EC3), refuses "." and "..".
  2. Refuses a second descriptor on another drive (byte +7).
  3. Copies the 11 name characters (+8 on) and checks the new name ($189E).
  4. Refuses a name that exists already, then finds the entry again and stores the name.
  Entry: DE = the file's descriptor (first byte $FE); BC = the descriptor holding the new name;
  HL = passed on to $12F9.
  Exit: A = 0 with Z.
  Error: NZ, or C with A = $AB, with A = the error code: $9E (Invalid . or .. operations), $AF
  (Invalid drive), $AB (Duplicate filename), one from $189E or $12F9.
  Changes: AF, BC, DE, HL, IX, AF', BC', DE', HL'; the directory.
  Keeps: IY.
  .1B4C Keeps HL
  .1B4D DE = the entry, IX = the descriptor, A = attributes
  .1B50 Not found
  .1B52 A directory?
  .1B54 Yes: clears the word at HL+$1E
  .1B57 Is it "." or ".."?
  .1B5A Yes: error $9E
  .1B5C The new name's descriptor
  .1B5E HL' = the file's descriptor
  .1B61 Its byte +7 (the drive)
  .1B65 DE' = the file's descriptor +7
  .1B66 HL' = the new name's descriptor
  .1B67 +7
  .1B68 The same drive?
  .1B6A Error code $AF (Invalid drive)
  .1B6C No: refused
  .1B6E 11 name characters

@1B70 REN_COPY_NAME
  Copies one character of the new name over the old one, except a "?" which keeps the old one.
  .1B72 A = the new character
  .1B73 A "?"?
  .1B75 Yes: keep the old one
  .1B77 No: store it

@1B78 REN_COPY_NEXT
  The next character; then checks the new name and its uniqueness and finds the entry again.
  .1B78 Next character
  .1B7B Is the new name valid?
  .1B7E No: refused
  .1B80 Starts at the directory's first entry
  .1B83 Searches it for the new name
  .1B86 HL = the caller's HL
  .1B87 Error code $AB (Duplicate filename)
  .1B89 Found: refused
  .1B8A DE = the file's descriptor
  .1B8D Finds its entry again
  .1B8E Not found
  .1B91 HL = the new name in the descriptor
  .1B98 Its first character
  .1B99 $E5?
  .1B9B No: as it is
  .1B9D $E5 is stored as $05 in an entry

@1B9F REN_STORE_NAME
  Stores the name's first character in the entry, then the other 10 through ENTRY_COPY_N.
  .1B9F The first character
  .1BA2 10 more
  .1BA4 Copies them and marks the buffer changed

@1BA7 ERR_ATTRIBUTES
  Leaves with error $A1 (Invalid file attributes).
  .1BA7 Error code $A1

@1BA9 FN_POP_RET
  The common error exit of functions 9 and 10: drops the HL kept on the stack and returns with
  the error code in A.
  .1BA9 Drops the kept HL

@1BAB FN10_MOVE
  EXDOS function 10 (table $0550): moves a file or a directory to another directory on the same
  drive. The entry is copied to $06A1 above IY, deleted ($E5) where it is, and written to a free
  entry of the destination; a moved directory's ".." entry is then pointed at its new parent.
  Volume labels and descriptors with bit 3 of +$06 set are refused.
  Entry: DE = the descriptor of what moves (first byte $FE); BC = the destination's path
  descriptor, read by $1369; HL = passed on to $12F9.
  Exit: A = 0 with Z.
  Error: NZ with A = the error code: $A1 (Invalid file attributes), $9E (Invalid . or ..
  operations), $A8 (Invalid pathname string), $AF (Invalid drive), $A9 (Invalid directory move),
  $AB (Duplicate filename), or one from $12F9, $1369 or $199A.
  Changes: AF, BC, DE, HL, IX, AF', BC', DE', HL'; the directories.
  Keeps: IY.
  .1BAB Keeps HL
  .1BAC DE = the entry, IX = the descriptor, A = attributes
  .1BAF Not found
  .1BB1 Descriptor bit 3 set?
  .1BB3 Yes: error $A1
  .1BB7 A volume label?
  .1BB9 Yes: error $A1
  .1BBB Keeps the destination
  .1BBC C = $81, the flags passed to $1369
  .1BBE A directory?
  .1BC0 No
  .1BC2 Adds flag bit 2 for a directory
  .1BC5 HL = the directory's first cluster
  .1BCA Kept at IY+$5E/$5F
  .1BD3 Clears the word at HL+$1E

@1BD6 FN10_CHECKS
  Function 10, after the source: refuses "." and "..", reads the destination path ($1369) and checks
  it (a plain path, the same drive, not inside the directory being moved, no entry of the same
  name), takes a free entry there, then moves the entry and fixes a moved directory's "..".
  .1BD6 Is the source "." or ".."?
  .1BD9 DE = the destination's descriptor
  .1BDA Yes: error $9E
  .1BDC Clears the flags at IY+$5D
  .1BE0 A' = the source's drive
  .1BE4 Reads the destination path
  .1BE7 Bad path
  .1BE9 Anything left in C?
  .1BEA Error code $A8 (Invalid pathname string)
  .1BEC Yes: refused
  .1BEE A = the destination's drive
  .1BEF The same drive?
  .1BF2 Error code $AF (Invalid drive)
  .1BF4 No: refused
  .1BF6 Destination inside the moved directory?
  .1BFA Error code $A9 (Invalid directory move)
  .1BFC Yes: refused
  .1BFE Starts at the destination's first entry
  .1C01 Searches it for the name
  .1C04 Error code $AB (Duplicate filename)
  .1C06 Found: refused
  .1C08 Finds a free entry
  .1C0B None
  .1C0D Records where the free entry is
  .1C10 HL = the caller's HL
  .1C11 DE = the source's descriptor
  .1C14 Finds the source entry again
  .1C17 Not found
  .1C18 A directory?
  .1C1A Kept for after the move
  .1C1B Yes: clears the word at HL+$1E
  .1C1E HL = IY, the caller's HL kept on the stack
  .1C21 $06A1 above IY: a 32-byte copy of the entry
  .1C27 HL = the source entry
  .1C28 One entry
  .1C2B Copies it
  .1C2D HL = the source entry
  .1C2E Deleted mark
  .1C30 Marks the buffer changed
  .1C33 DE = the copy
  .1C34 HL = the caller's HL
  .1C36 Back to the free entry found at $1C08
  .1C39 DE = the free entry
  .1C3C HL = the copy, the caller's HL on the stack
  .1C3D One entry
  .1C41 Writes it there
  .1C43 Marks the buffer changed
  .1C48 Z: not a directory
  .1C49 A = 0
  .1C4A A file: done
  .1C4B BC = the destination directory's cluster
  .1C52 IX+8 = "..", a descriptor for the search
  .1C56 Enters the moved directory
  .1C59 Its first entry
  .1C5C Searches it for ".."
  .1C5F BC = the new parent's cluster
  .1C60 Not found: done
  .1C61 A directory?
  .1C63 No: done
  .1C64 HL = the ".." entry's first cluster
  .1C68 Points it at the new parent
  .1C6B Marks the buffer changed

@1C6D FN11_SET_ATTR
  EXDOS function 11 (table $0550): stores a file's attributes and its time and date from its
  descriptor (attributes at +$13, time and date at +$1E-$21) into its directory entry. Some bits
  may not change: none of a volume label's, all but bit 1 (hidden) of a directory's, and bits 7,
  6, 4 and 3 of a file's.
  Entry: DE = the file's descriptor (first byte $FE); HL = passed on to $12F9.
  Exit: A = 0 with Z.
  Error: NZ with A = $A1 (Invalid file attributes), or the error from $12F9.
  Changes: AF, BC, DE, HL, IX, AF', BC', DE', HL'; the directory.
  Keeps: IY.
  .1C6D Keeps the descriptor
  .1C6E DE = the entry, A = its attributes
  .1C71 HL = the descriptor
  .1C72 Not found
  .1C73 +$13: the new attributes
  .1C77 DE = them, HL = the entry
  .1C7A HL = the entry's attributes
  .1C7B B = $FF: no bit may change
  .1C7C A volume label?
  .1C7E Yes: B = $FF
  .1C80 Only bit 1 may change
  .1C82 A directory?
  .1C84 Yes: B = $FD
  .1C86 A file: bits 0, 1, 2 and 5 may change

@1C88 ATTR_CHECK
  Refuses attributes that change a bit outside the allowed ones (B = the fixed bits); stores them,
  then copies the time and date.
  .1C88 A = the new attributes
  .1C8A Which bits change?
  .1C8B Any fixed one?
  .1C8C Error code $A1 (Invalid file attributes)
  .1C8E Yes: refused
  .1C8F Stores the attributes
  .1C93 HL = the entry's time
  .1C94 DE = it
  .1C95 HL = the descriptor's time, +$1E
  .1C96 Time and date: 4 bytes

@1C98 ENTRY_COPY_N
  Copies BC bytes from HL to DE into the directory entry, then marks the buffer changed.

@1C9A ENTRY_CHANGED
  Marks the current sector buffer changed and returns A = 0 with Z.
  .1C9A Marks the buffer changed
  .1C9D A = 0: done

@1C9F DISK_IO
  The floppy controller driver: runs one operation on a drive of the disk card. The controller's
  ports are found from the segment this ROM runs in (port $B3, page 3's segment, halved; $10 when
  bit 1 of that is set): base+0 command/status, +1 track, +2 sector, +3 data, base+8 the drive
  latch (selects, side, bit 5 from H; reads back DRQ in bit 7, INTRQ in bit 1, a disk change in
  bit 6). Each drive's track is kept at IY-$1B+n (n = 0-3), $FF when not known.
  A = 0: forgets the tracks of the drives in B (B = 0: also stops a busy command and resets the
  latch). 1 reads sectors, 2 writes sectors, 3 verifies sectors against the buffer, 4 writes a
  track, 5 reads a track, 6 reads an address field, 7 only reports a disk change, 8 restores the
  drive in B and counts its tracks. Another code gives $80.
  Entry: A = the operation; B bits 0-3 = the drive (its lowest set bit); C bit 0 = the side; D = the
  track ($FF: stay); E = the first sector; H bits 0-1 = the step rate, bit 2 = write
  precompensation, bit 3 = double step, bit 5 = to the latch; L = the sector count; IX = the buffer.
  Exit: A = 0 with Z when done; L = the sectors done; B as on entry except after a disk change; for operation 8 A = 0 with L = $FF (49 or more
  steps back to track 0) or 0, A = 1 when track 0 was not found.
  Error: NZ with A = 1 (time-out), 2 (verify mismatch), $20 (the disk was changed: B = the drives
  marked changed), $80 (bad operation or no drive), or the controller's error bits (status AND
  $5C: $40 write protected, $10 not found, $08 CRC, $04 lost data).
  Changes: AF, B, L; IY-$1F to IY-$13, the 4 bytes at IY-$4A, IY-$5F.
  Keeps: C, DE, H, IX, IY.
  .1C9F Keeps the operation
  .1CA2 Keeps the side
  .1CA5 Keeps the sector
  .1CA8 Keeps the track
  .1CAC DE = the buffer
  .1CAF Keeps the buffer as the exit's IX
  .1CB1 Port $B3: page 3's segment
  .1CB3 C = this ROM's segment
  .1CB5 Halved: the controller's port base
  .1CB7 Segment bit 2 set?
  .1CBB Yes: base $10

@1CBD DIO_DISPATCH
  Runs the operation in A: 0 (DIO_RESET), 1-7 (DIO_TRANSFER), 8 (DIO_COUNT_TRK), others fail.
  .1CBD Operation 0?
  .1CC0 Restarts the count at IY-$5F
  .1CC4 1-7?
  .1CC9 8?

@1CCB DIO_BAD_OP
  An operation above 8 (or no drive given): A = $80, L = 0.
  .1CCB Result $80
  .1CCD No sectors done

@1CCF DIO_EXIT
  The exit: puts back BC, IX and DE, and sets the flags from the result in A.
  .1CCF The caller's BC

@1CD0 DIO_EXIT_B
  The exit that keeps B as set (a disk change): puts back IX and DE, flags from A.
  .1CD0 The caller's IX
  .1CD2 The caller's DE
  .1CD3 Z when A = 0

@1CD5 DIO_RESET
  Operation 0: sets the track of every drive in B's low 4 bits to $FF (not known).
  .1CD5 A = the drives
  .1CD8 IY is stepped per drive

@1CDA DIO_FORGET_LOOP
  One drive bit per pass, drive 0 first.
  .1CDA This drive's bit
  .1CDC Not given
  .1CDE Its track is not known

@1CE2 DIO_FORGET_NEXT
  Steps to the next drive while bits are left; then stops a busy command when B = 0.
  .1CE2 The next drive's track byte
  .1CE4 More drives
  .1CE8 Controller status
  .1CEA Busy?
  .1CEB No
  .1CED Force interrupt
  .1CEF Stops the command

@1CF1 DIO_RESET_DONE
  With drives given, returns A = 0. With B = 0 also clears the latch copy at IY-$1C, writes the
  latch (drive 0 selected only while the motor runs) and sets the count at IY-$5F to $0A.
  .1CF1 A = 0
  .1CF2 Drives given?
  .1CF3 Yes: done
  .1CF5 No latch value
  .1CF8 Controller status
  .1CFA Motor on?
  .1CFC A = 1 when it is
  .1CFD The drive latch
  .1CFF Selects drive 0 only while the motor runs
  .1D01 The count at IY-$5F
  .1D05 A = 0: done

@1D08 DIO_COUNT_TRK
  Operation 8: restores the drive in B, steps in 50 tracks and counts the steps out back to track
  0. 49 or more gives L = $FF, fewer L = 0; no track 0 within 256 steps gives A = 1. With no drive
  in B it restores drive 0, and drive 1 too when drive 0 reports track 0 (DIO_RESTORE_0_1).
  .1D08 Flags: the motor counts as running
  .1D0C A = the lowest drive bit
  .1D12 None: restore drives 0 and 1
  .1D14 Selects and restores the drive
  .1D17 Waits
  .1D1A 50 steps in

@1D1C DIO_STEP_IN
  Steps in one track per pass, then waits.
  .1D1C Step in, update track
  .1D1E Issues it and waits
  .1D24 Waits

@1D27 DIO_STEP_OUT
  Steps out one track per pass until track 0, counting the steps in D; A = 1 after 256 steps.
  .1D27 Step out, update track
  .1D29 Issues it; NZ at track 0
  .1D2C Track 0
  .1D2E One more step
  .1D2F Up to 256
  .1D31 Track 0 not found

@1D35 DIO_TRACK_ZERO
  Track 0 reached after D steps out: L = $FF for 49 or more, L = 0 for fewer; A = 0.
